Done — focused refactoring of the `org.springframework.boot.json` package.

## What I refactored

**`core/spring-boot/src/main/java/org/springframework/boot/json/JsonValueWriter.java`** — removed duplicated logic between two pairs of methods:

```java
// before
<E> void writeArray(Consumer<Consumer<E>> elements) {
    start(Series.ARRAY);
    elements.accept(ThrowingConsumer.of(this::writeElement));  // == writeElements' body
    end(Series.ARRAY);
}
<N, V> void writeObject(Consumer<BiConsumer<N, V>> pairs) {
    start(Series.OBJECT);
    pairs.accept(this::writePair);                              // == writePairs' body
    end(Series.OBJECT);
}

// after
<E> void writeArray(Consumer<Consumer<E>> elements) {
    start(Series.ARRAY);
    writeElements(elements);
    end(Series.ARRAY);
}
<N, V> void writeObject(Consumer<BiConsumer<N, V>> pairs) {
    start(Series.OBJECT);
    writePairs(pairs);
    end(Series.OBJECT);
}
```

`writeArray` was re-implementing `writeElements` and `writeObject` was re-implementing `writePairs`. Now each "write a whole series" method is expressed as `start → write-contents → end`, reusing the "write into an already-started series" method. Behavior is identical because the delegated method bodies are character-for-character what was inlined.

## What I deliberately left alone in this package

I reviewed the whole package, and want to be explicit about what I *didn't* touch and why:

- **`JsonValueWriter.append(String)` / `append(char)`** — these look like duplication but are a deliberate hot-path pattern: keeping them separate hits the right `Appendable.append` overload without boxing a `char` into a `String`. Merging them would add a per-call allocation.
- **`MemberPath` "parent-or-root" idiom** (`x.parent() != null ? x.parent() : ROOT`, used twice) — extracting it would mean adding a method to `MemberPath`, which is a *public-API* record (exposed via the `NameProcessor`/`ValueProcessor` callbacks). I won't expand public API surface for an internal convenience.
- **`AbstractJsonParser`, `BasicJsonParser`, `Gson`/`Jackson` parsers, `JsonParserFactory`, `AppendableByteArray`, `WritableJson`, `MemberPath` parsing** — all clean / allocation-sensitive; nothing worth changing.

So this package yielded exactly one genuine, safe improvement, and that's what I applied.

Same standing caveat as the others: reasoned-only (verified by reading the code + confirming `JsonValueWriterTests`/`JsonWriterTests` cover arrays and objects), so it still needs the project's Gradle + checkstyle/`spring-javaformat` gates before merge.

